; Real literals denote their HASH_V0-normalized value (spec/serialization.md):
; -0.0 is +0.0 in expressions and quoted code, so hash-equal programs run the
; same numbers in both engines (the first two components). Literal patterns are
; normalized too, but only representationally: matching already treats signed
; zeros as equal, so the third component documents behaviour rather than
; discriminating it. A computed negative zero keeps its sign (the fourth).
(tuple
  (app (var code.render) (quote (lit -0.0)))
  (app (var real.div) (lit 1.0) (lit -0.0))
  (match (lit 0.0) (clause (plit -0.0) (lit "matched")) (clause (pwild) (lit "missed")))
  (app (var real.div) (lit 1.0) (app (var real.mul) (lit -1.0) (lit 0.0))))
